🏆 The Ultimate 100-Rule UI Design System for AI Agents

## SECTION 1: General Layout & Spacing (1-15)
1. **Use a strict 8-point grid system** for all margins, padding, and element sizing (8, 16, 24, 32, etc.).
2. **Never center-align text blocks** that exceed 3 lines; always left-align for readability.
3. **Use generous whitespace (negative space)** to group related elements and separate unrelated ones.
4. **Establish a clear visual hierarchy:** The most important element must be the largest or most contrasting.
5. **Keep primary actions above the fold** (visible without scrolling).
6. **Use a maximum of 3 distinct content column widths** per screen to avoid visual chaos.
7. **Align all elements to a baseline grid** to create a subconscious feeling of order.
8. **Ensure touch targets** (buttons, icons) are a minimum of 44x44px for mobile accessibility.
9. **Limit line length** to 45–75 characters per line for optimal reading comfort.
10. **Use the F-pattern or Z-pattern** layout logic for pages with heavy text or forms.
11. **Group related items using the Gestalt principle of Proximity** (put them close together).
12. **Avoid full-width text forms** on desktop; cap form widths at ~600px.
13. **Use progressive disclosure** (like Accordions) to hide complex information until requested.
14. **Maintain consistent margins** between sections (e.g., all section breaks are 48px apart).
15. **Design for thumb zones** on mobile (keep primary nav at the bottom).

## SECTION 2: Typography & Readability (16-30)
16. **Use maximum 2 font families per project** (e.g., one Sans-Serif for UI, one Serif for headings).
17. **Use clean, modern Sans-Serif fonts** (Inter, Poppins, Manrope, Roboto, SF Pro).
18. **Never use decorative, script, or novelty fonts** for UI copy.
19. **Ensure a minimum font size of 16px** for body text on mobile to prevent zooming.
20. **Use a line-height (leading) of 1.4 to 1.6** for body text; 1.1 to 1.3 for headlines.
21. **Limit font weights:** Stick to Regular (400), Medium (500), and Bold (700).
22. **Use letterspacing (tracking) to tighten large headlines** and open up small uppercase text.
23. **Ensure a contrast ratio of at least 4.5:1** for normal text (WCAG AA standard).
24. **Left-align long paragraphs;** center-align only short headlines or single lines.
25. **Avoid all-caps** for long strings of text; use them only for small labels or tags.
26. **Use color and weight, not just size,** to create typographic hierarchy.
27. **Keep paragraph spacing distinct** (usually equal to the line height) to separate blocks of text.
28. **Use truncation (ellipsis ...)** for text that overflows, rather than breaking layouts.
29. **Avoid orphan words** (a single word on the last line of a paragraph) in hero sections.
30. **Design for dark mode typography:** Use off-white text (e.g., #E0E0E0) instead of pure white (#FFF) on dark backgrounds to reduce eye strain.

## SECTION 3: Color Theory & Contrast (31-45)
31. **Use the 60-30-10 rule:** 60% dominant neutral, 30% secondary color, 10% accent (action) color.
32. **Limit saturation in Dark Mode:** Use muted, softer tones rather than neon or highly saturated primary colors.
33. **Use color purposefully:** Red for errors, Green for success, Yellow for warnings.
34. **Never rely on color alone** to convey meaning (add icons or text labels for accessibility).
35. **Ensure pure black (#000000) is avoided** in backgrounds; use dark greys (e.g., #121212) for premium dark UIs.
36. **Use subtle gradients** to add depth to flat designs, especially in headers or cards.
37. **Add a slight tint of your brand color** to your neutral greys to unify the UI.
38. **Keep buttons high contrast** against their background.
39. **Use color to guide the eye:** Only use your primary accent color for the most important interactive elements.
40. **Avoid pure white (#FFFFFF)** for large background areas; use off-white (e.g., #F8F9FA) to reduce glare.
41. **Check color contrast for disabled states** (ensure they are distinguishable but clearly inactive).
42. **Use blur and translucency (Glassmorphism)** sparingly to create layered depth.
43. **Limit your UI color palette** to 1 Primary, 1 Secondary, 4 Neutrals (shades of grey), and Semantic Colors.
44. **Ensure hover states** change the color slightly (e.g., darken by 10%) to provide feedback.
45. **Test your UI in greyscale** to ensure the hierarchy still works without color.

## SECTION 4: Components & UI Elements (46-65)
46. **Buttons:** Include distinct states (Default, Hover, Active, Focus, Disabled, Loading).
47. **Forms:** Always use **vertical alignment** (Label above the input field).
48. **Forms:** Avoid placeholders as labels; they disappear when the user types.
49. **Dropdowns:** Do not use dropdowns if there are only 2-3 options; display them directly (Radio buttons).
50. **Search:** Allow users to both type and scroll through long lists (e.g., country selectors).
51. **Loading States:** Use **Skeleton Loading** instead of spinning circular loaders.
52. **Loading States:** Use progressive loading (load text first, then images).
53. **Modals:** Always include a clear "Close" (X) button and an escape route (Cancel).
54. **Modals:** Dim the background (using a 40-60% black overlay) to focus attention.
55. **Toggles:** Ensure the "On" state is clearly distinct from the "Off" state (color + icon shift).
56. **Checkboxes vs. Radios:** Use checkboxes for multiple selections, radio buttons for single selections.
57. **Breadcrumbs:** Use them for deep navigation hierarchies (Home > Category > Sub-category).
58. **Tooltips:** Use them only for supplementary information, never for critical instructions.
59. **Navigation:** Highlight the active tab/page in the navigation menu.
60. **Empty States:** Always design a specific screen for when there is no data (e.g., "No messages yet").
61. **Empty States:** Include a clear Call to Action (CTA) in the empty state to guide the next step.
62. **Toasts/Snackbars:** Place them at the bottom or top of the screen and auto-dismiss after 3-5 seconds.
63. **Icons:** Ensure all icons share the same stroke width and visual style.
64. **Icons:** Pair icons with text labels; do not rely on icons alone for complex actions.
65. **Data Tables:** Ensure the header row is visually distinct (bold, sticky on scroll).

## SECTION 5: The 10 Golden Rules of Card Design (66-75)
66. **Grouping:** Only group related information into a single card. Do not mix unrelated data.
67. **Containment:** Use a subtle border (1px) or a soft drop shadow to define the card's boundaries.
68. **Corner Radius:** Keep the border-radius consistent across all cards (e.g., 12px or 16px).
69. **Internal Padding:** Ensure padding inside the card is generous (min 16px-24px) so content doesn't touch the edges.
70. **Visual Hierarchy within Cards:** The title is largest/boldest, subtitle is smaller, data is prominent.
71. **Actionability:** If a card is clickable, make sure the whole card has a hover state (e.g., slight lift or shadow increase).
72. **Interactive Data Cards:** Use simple visuals (like mini pie charts or line graphs) to make numbers easier to understand without context.
73. **Image Aspect Ratios:** Keep images within cards uniform (e.g., 16:9 or 1:1) to prevent jagged layouts.
74. **Scannability:** Use bold text for key numbers (e.g., **$145.32**) so users can scan data quickly.
75. **Card Overload:** Do not put too many CTAs inside one card. Limit it to one primary action.

## SECTION 6: UX & Cognitive Load (76-90)
76. **Fitts's Law:** Make important buttons large and easily clickable.
77. **Hick's Law:** Reduce the number of choices to speed up decision-making.
78. **Miller's Law:** Chunk information into groups of 5-7 items (e.g., phone number formatting: 555-019-2834).
79. **Jakob's Law:** Users prefer your site to work the same way as all the other sites they already know.
80. **Feedback:** Always provide immediate visual feedback when a user interacts with an element.
81. **Error Prevention:** Disable the "Submit" button until required fields are filled out.
82. **Error Messages:** Place error text immediately below the field, in red, with an icon.
83. **Error Messages:** Explain *how to fix* the error, not just that an error occurred.
84. **Form Autofill:** Design forms to support browser autofill (properly named inputs).
85. **Microcopy:** Write clear, human-readable button text (e.g., "Create Account" instead of "Submit").
86. **Microcopy:** Keep instructions in the active voice and concise.
87. **Exit Points:** Always let users undo actions or go back easily.
88. **Skeuomorphism:** Use subtle real-world depth (shadows, gradients) only to clarify clickability.
89. **Consistency:** If a button looks the same, it must act the same across the entire app.
90. **Accessibility:** Ensure all interactive elements are focusable via keyboard (Tab navigation).

## SECTION 7: Advanced Styling & Aesthetics (91-100)
91. **Shadows:** Use layered shadows (multiple shadows stacked) for a realistic, premium depth effect.
92. **Shadows:** Shadows should be soft, large, and low opacity (e.g., `rgba(0,0,0,0.05)`).
93. **Borders:** Use borders subtly (e.g., `#E5E7EB`) to separate elements instead of heavy shadows.
94. **Glassmorphism:** Use background blur (`backdrop-filter: blur(10px)`) with a semi-transparent white/black background for overlays.
95. **Dark Mode Depth:** Use lighter shades of your background color to create elevation (e.g., a card is a lighter grey than the background).
96. **Immersive Visuals:** Use full-bleed images with gradient overlays for text readability.
97. **Illustrations:** Use consistent illustration styles (e.g., all 3D or all flat vector) across empty states and onboarding.
98. **Motion:** Keep animations between 200ms and 500ms; use ease-in-out timing functions.
99. **Motion:** Animate elements that appear (fade in, slide up) to provide spatial context.
100. **Polish:** Ensure everything aligns. A 1px misalignment makes the entire design feel cheap.
